.TH LSPFS 4
.SH NAME
lspfs \- language servers as files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lspfs
[
.B -D
] [
.B -m
.I mtpt
] [
.B -s
.I srvname
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Lspfs
runs language servers, programs that speak the Language Server Protocol,
and serves what they know as files.
It posts itself as
.BI /srv/ srvname
.RB ( /srv/lsp
by default) and mounts itself on
.I mtpt
.RB ( /mnt/lsp ).
It knows nothing of an editor: a question names a file and a place in it, in runes,
and the answer is lines of text.
.PP
Which server serves a file, and how, is given by rules, read from
.B $home/lib/lsp
or, if there is none,
.BR /lib/lsp .
They are
.IR ndb (6)
entries.
An entry
.B lsp=
.I name
gives a server:
.TF func=
.TP
.B cmd=
the command that runs it, by
.IR rc (1)
.TP
.B file=
a regular expression
.RI ( regexp (6))
for the full names of the files it serves; the first rule that matches serves a file
.TP
.B lang=
the language's LSP name, the server's name if not given
.TP
.B root=
the names of the files that mark a project's top directory, in order.
For each name in turn,
.I lspfs
looks in the file's directory and then each above it;
the first name found anywhere gives the root.
A server with no
.B root=
gets no project at all and finds its own.
.TP
.B func=
the pattern a function's first line matches, for
.IR fn (1)
.TP
.B put=
what to do to a buffer after it is put:
.L fmt
formats it, and other words are kinds of code action to apply
.PD
.PP
An entry
.B tree=
.I path
makes
.I path
the root of the files under it, whatever their markers say, for every server or, with
.BI lsp= name\f1,
for that one.
Of two trees, the nearer wins.
.PP
.I Lspfs
starts a server the first time a file it serves is opened or asked about,
and runs one process for all the projects it serves, telling it of each new root.
A server that cannot be told of another project gets a process for each.
A server that exits is started again, and the documents it had are opened in it again;
one that exits five times within three minutes is not started again until the rules are
written.
Each runs in a directory of its own in
.BR /tmp ,
with its errors in
.BI /tmp/lsp. name .err\f1.
.SS Questions
The files
.BR def ,
.BR type ,
.BR refs ,
.BR impls ,
.BR doc ,
.B comp
and
.B syms
each answer a question.
Open one for reading and writing, write a file's full name, a colon and a
.LR # ,
then a place in runes, as in
.LR /usr/glenda/src/a.fdn:#120 ,
and read the answer.
.B Syms
takes the file's name alone.
The answer starts where the write leaves the offset, so
.IP
.EX
{ echo -n '/usr/glenda/src/a.fdn:#120' >[1=0]; cat } <>/mnt/lsp/def
.EE
.PP
prints it.
.BR Def ,
.BR type ,
.B refs
and
.B impls
give places, one a line, as
.IB file : line : col :\f1
and the line's text;
.B doc
gives the diagnostics on the place's line and then what the server says of it;
.B comp
gives the completions, one a line;
.B syms
gives the file's symbols, a line each, as a place, the kind of symbol and its name, indented
under the symbol they are in.
A file that no client has open is read from disk, again for each question.
.SS Documents
A client opens its text by
.BR docs/new :
reading it makes a directory,
.BI docs/ n\f1,
and gives
.IR n .
Each holds:
.TF tokens
.TP
.B name
the file's full name, written once
.TP
.B body
the text: what is written replaces it when the file is closed
.TP
.B edit
edits, each write one of acme's per-window log entries:
.BI "I " "q0 q1 " "0 " "n text"
inserts the
.I n
runes of
.I text
at
.IR q0 ,
and
.BI "D " "q0 q1"
deletes
.RI [ q0 , q1 )
.TP
.B tokens
the semantic tokens, a line each: a start and length in runes and a name, such as
.B keyword
or
.BR type ,
as
.IR astyles (4)
takes them
.TP
.B diag
the diagnostics, a line each:
.IB file : line : col :\f1
a severity
.RB ( error ,
.BR warning ,
.B info
or
.BR hint )
and the message
.PD
.PP
Writing two numbers to
.B tokens
or
.B diag
and reading gives only what is between them.
Removing
.BI docs/ n
closes the document.
.SS Log
.B Log
gives a line for each change, as it happens:
.IB n " new" \f1,
.IB n " del" \f1,
and
.IB n " tokens " "q0 q1"
or
.IB n " diag " "q0 q1"
when a document's tokens or diagnostics change between
.I q0
and
.IR q1 .
A reader rereads only that part.
.SS Rules
Reading
.B rules
gives the rules in use.
Writing it replaces them when it is closed, as the plumber's do.
A write with a bad entry fails, with the entry's line, and so do the writes after it; the rules
are then left as they were.
An entry the next line might still finish, one missing
.B cmd=
or
.BR file= ,
is checked only when the file is closed; if it is bad then, the rules are left as they were
and the error goes to standard error.
The servers then start again, by the new rules.
.IP
.EX
cp $home/lib/lsp /mnt/lsp/rules
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF $home/lib/lsp
.TP
.B /lib/lsp
the rules
.TP
.B $home/lib/lsp
the user's rules, read in place of
.B /lib/lsp
.PD
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/lspfs
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR astyles (4),
.IR acme (4),
.IR ndb (6)
.br
The Language Server Protocol,
.B https://microsoft.github.io/language-server-protocol/
